<Doc
  title="Coming from Mastra"
  description="How mcpd differs from Mastra's observability: it receives OpenTelemetry rather than exporting it, redacts all six signals not just spans, and samples deterministically. Plus what it does not do."
  lede="mcpd is a free, self-hosted alternative to Mastra's observability and to its durable workflows. This page says where it is ahead, where it is behind, and how to move."
  og="docs"
>
  <h2>What this is</h2>
  <p>
    Mastra is an agent framework: agents, workflows, memory, RAG and
    observability, in one TypeScript dependency. mcpd is a daemon beside your
    code: it holds a workflow between its steps and records every step, and
    it leaves the models, the prompts and the tools to you. It is not a
    framework around your agent, and it is written to stay out of one.
  </p>
  <p>
    That difference decides most of what follows. Mastra's observability knows
    about your agent because it is inside it. mcpd knows about your agent
    because your agent emits OpenTelemetry, which means it also works for the
    services around your agent, and for agents that were never written in
    TypeScript.
  </p>

  <h2>The differences that matter</h2>

  <h3>The workflow's bodies stay in your process, in any language</h3>
  <p>
    Both run durable workflows. Mastra's steps are TypeScript functions inside
    the Mastra process, suspended and resumed through its storage. mcpd's
    definition is data the daemon holds, and its step bodies are code your
    process holds, claimed one lease at a time through an HTTP protocol that
    six SDKs speak and any language can. The daemon never sees a model, a
    prompt or a credential, and a body can change without the definition
    changing.
  </p>
  <p>
    What the daemon does hold is the part that has to survive: the cursor, the
    journal, the retry budget, the timers, and a wait for an event that may
    take a week to arrive. A run outlives the process that started it and the
    worker that served its last step. See
    <a href="/docs/workflows" class="link">Workflows</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>It receives OpenTelemetry rather than exporting it from one framework</h3>
  <p>
    This is the whole thesis. Mastra's observability exports from inside a
    Mastra application, so what it can see is what that application does, in
    the language it is written in. mcpd is the collector: it receives OTLP from
    any OpenTelemetry SDK, in any language, with no mcpd code in your
    application, and records the services around the agent as readily as the
    agent.
  </p>
  <p>
    It also emits OTLP, so mcpd can sit in the middle of a pipeline you already
    have rather than at the end of one you have to build. See
    <a href="/docs/forwarding" class="link">Forwarding</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Redaction covers all six signals</h3>
  <p>
    Mastra's <code>SensitiveDataFilter</code> is a span processor. It runs on
    spans and nothing else, so a key passed to a structured log, or a secret
    quoted in a score's reason or a piece of feedback, reaches every exporter
    unredacted.
  </p>
  <p>
    mcpd redacts spans, logs, metric labels, scores, feedback and aspect notes,
    on by default, before anything is written and before anything is
    forwarded.
  </p>
  <p>
    Its key matching is also a substring rather than an exact name, so
    <code>awsSecretAccessKey</code> and <code>X-API-Key</code> are caught by the
    rules for <code>secret</code> and <code>x-api-key</code>. A rule set that
    requires the whole name to match misses both.
  </p>
  <p>
    Be clear about the limit: redaction matches on the name of a field, not on
    the shape of its contents. A token pasted into the middle of a prompt, or
    into a URL stored under a harmless key, is not removed by either tool.
    Redaction reduces what you leak by accident. It is not a guarantee.
  </p>
  <p>
    What mcpd adds for that case is a scorer rather than a filter. The built-in
    <code>no_leaked_secret</code> reads a run's answer and matches on the shape
    of a credential, so a key that made it into a completion is at least
    reported to you. See <a href="/docs/testing" class="link">Regression
    testing</a>.
  </p>

  <PipelineDiagram class="not-prose my-8" />

  <h3>Sampling is deterministic</h3>
  <p>
    Mastra's ratio sampling calls <code>Math.random()</code>. Every replica
    behind a load balancer therefore decides differently about the same trace,
    and a run that crosses two services can be kept by one and dropped by the
    other.
  </p>
  <p>
    mcpd hashes the trace id, so every instance reaches the same answer with no
    coordination and a kept trace is kept whole. The daemon's flags reach two
    strategies, keep everything and this deterministic fraction; a tail strategy
    that would keep every error whatever the rate says exists in the policy
    type and is not reachable from the command line in this build. See
    <a href="/docs/sampling" class="link">Sampling</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The rest, briefly</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Area</th><th>What mcpd does</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Deployment</td>
        <td>One static binary with the store and the interface compiled in. No Node, no sidecar.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Storage</td>
        <td>SQLite for a laptop, Postgres or ClickHouse for a team. All three tested against a real server in CI.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Search</td>
        <td>Full text over every stored prompt and completion.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Metrics</td>
        <td>Aggregated at ingest, with exact quantiles and exemplars back to a run.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Cost</td>
        <td>Priced at ingest and stored. Cached tokens charged at their own rate. An unknown model is left unpriced rather than reported as free.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>SDKs</td>
        <td>TypeScript, Python, Rust, Go, Kotlin and Elixir, generated from one specification so they cannot drift. Each carries a workflow builder and a worker.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Workflows</td>
        <td>Nine constructs, held by the daemon: steps, agent turns, sleeps, waits on events, parallel branches, foreach, bounded loops and child runs. Retries, effects and limits are per step. A conformance suite serves the same run from all six languages.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Evaluation</td>
        <td>Thirteen deterministic scorers, plus model-graded scoring against any OpenAI-shaped endpoint. Datasets captured from production runs, and experiment comparison that reports its sample size.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Licence</td>
        <td>Apache-2.0. Self-hosted with no account, or free hosted at mcpd.sh.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Where mcpd is behind</h2>
  <p>
    Stated here rather than left for you to discover, because a comparison that
    only lists wins is an advertisement.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Not a framework around your code.</strong> Mastra gives you
      agents, memory, RAG and a model router in one dependency. mcpd gives you
      the durable run and the record of it, and leaves models, prompts and
      tools to your code. If you want one dependency that does everything,
      Mastra is the one dependency.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No named vendor exporters.</strong> Mastra ships integrations for
      Langfuse, LangSmith, Braintrust, Arize, Datadog and others by name. mcpd
      forwards OTLP, which reaches most of them through one implementation, but
      a destination that speaks only its own protocol is out of reach.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No SSO, SCIM or RBAC.</strong> Authentication is six API key
      scopes and a project list per key. Put it behind the identity proxy you
      already run.
    </li>
  </ul>


  <h2>Moving over</h2>
  <p>
    If your agent already emits OpenTelemetry, this is two environment
    variables. mcpd speaks OTLP over HTTP, on the endpoints every
    OpenTelemetry SDK already knows how to talk to. Traces can be JSON or
    protobuf; logs and metrics must be JSON.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="point an existing OpenTelemetry setup at mcpd"
    command={`export OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4317
export OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL=http/json`}
  />

  <p>
    The protocol line matters. Port 4317 is conventionally gRPC, and mcpd speaks
    HTTP on it, so an SDK left on its gRPC default connects to the right port
    and fails. <a href="/docs/opentelemetry" class="link">OpenTelemetry</a> goes
    through this and the rest of the settings.
  </p>

  <p>
    Run both for a fortnight. mcpd forwards the spans it stores on to whatever
    you were using before, so you can compare the two on the same traces rather
    than on a migration you cannot undo. It forwards spans only: logs, metrics,
    scores, feedback and aspect notes stay in mcpd.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="keep the old destination while you decide"
    command="mcpd serve --forward-endpoint https://your-existing-collector"
  />

  <p>
    If your agent does not emit OpenTelemetry yet, start with
    <a href="/docs/quickstart" class="link">Quickstart</a>, or pick your
    language under Instrument in the sidebar.
  </p>

  <h2>How this comparison was made</h2>
  <p>
    The Mastra claims on this page come from reading its source in August 2026,
    not from its documentation and not from running a benchmark. There are no
    performance numbers here because we have not measured any we would stand
    behind.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mastra is actively developed and any of this can stop being true. If you
    find something out of date, it is a bug in this page:
    <a href="https://github.com/mcpd/mcpd/issues" class="link">open an issue</a>
    and it will be corrected.
  </p>

